      "field_id": "B.clinical_phenotypes",
      "value": "Loss of acquired purposeful hand use followed by hand-wringing stereotypies; developmental regression with stabilization phase; epilepsy (60-90%, onset usually 3-10 years); postnatal head growth deceleration / microcephaly; early hypotonia; loss of acquired speech; gait abnormality; scoliosis; waking breathing abnormalities; bruxism; sleep rhythm disturbance. FOXG1 congenital variant: severe microcephaly, repetitive tongue protrusion, irregular violent upper limb movements, strabismus, frontotemporal hypoplasia, delayed myelination, corpus callosum hypoplasia",
      "candidates": [],
      "source": "article",
      "evaluation_type": "precision_recall"
    },
    {
      "field_id": "B.age_of_onset",
      "value": "Typical Rett: apparently normal to 6-18 months then regression; early seizure variant (CDKL5): seizure onset 1 week to 5 months; congenital variant (FOXG1): severe developmental delay from early infancy",
